the protest started peacefully  with demonstrators chanting  pro-democracy songs  there was heavy police presence some  officers even moving alongside the small  crowd  until the first cordon was breached  then the security forces lobbed rounds  of tear gas at the protesters  the crowd including dozens of  journalists had to run  to find safety police then fired  gunshots  into the air bengal  who participated in the first june 12  movement 28 years ago  says the ideals they fought for have  been destroyed  by the political class that's why  they're back on the streets  nigerians saw it a duty to themselves  and to their generation yes  to begin to come out a mass you're like  we came out to the decade  that started with june 12 1993 to come  out time to begin to say no to this  nonsense that is going on  the june 12 movement was born after the  annulment of an election in 1993  arguably the freest in nigeria's history  the winner of that vote was later  arrested by the military  and died in detention what followed were  protests  and violence that grouped the nation  forcing the military  to organize another transfer of power to  civilians  six years later that transition to  civilian power  is marked here every year as democracy  day  but some activists who took part in last  year's anti-police brutality protest  that turned violent at times stayed away  this year  saying street protests like this can  easily be hijacked by opportunists  nigeria is sitting on the keg of  gunpowder and anything  can we can make it explode so i am not  on the street because i feel that  as a nigerian one my loyalties with my  country  protests were also reported in other  cities including the capital abuja  where pro and anti-government protesters  clashed  ahead of the protest security forces  have been deployed in large numbers here  in lagos  and across nigeria the protests are  coming at a time  when nigerians are angry at politicians  for failing  to rein in widespread violence  corruption inflation  and poverty worried about the repeat of  the anti-police brutality protest last  year that resulted in widespread looting  in arson  many businesses in lagos remained closed  on saturday  earlier in a nationwide broadcast to  mark the day president muhammad buhari  acknowledged existing challenges facing  nigeria  he assured the public the nation's  democracy will continue to evolve  until such challenges are met but  millions of citizens  burdened by inflation economic  stagnation  and violence say the change must happen  now ahamad idris al jazeera  lagos nigeria
 

